=Final decision=
''All numbering based on [[/Proposed decision]], where vote counts and comments are also available.''
==Principles==
===Pillars===
1) Wikipedia articles must be [[WP:NPOV|neutral]], [[WP:V|verifiable]] and must not contain [[WP:OR|original research]].  Those founding principles (the ''[[WP:5P|Pillars]]'') are not negotiable and cannot be overruled, even when apparent consensus to do so exists.

:''Passed 10 to 0, 22:34, 24 August 2010 (UTC)''

===Original research===
2) Wikipedia defines "original research" as ''"facts, allegations, ideas, and stories not already published by reliable sources"''. In particular, analyses or conclusions not already published in reliable, third-party, published sources with a reputation for fact-checking and accuracy are not appropriate for inclusion in articles.

:''Passed 10 to 0, 22:34, 24 August 2010 (UTC)''

===Correct use of sources===
3.1) Wikipedia articles should be based on [[WP:PSTS|reliable, published secondary sources]] and, to a lesser extent, on tertiary sources. Primary sources are permitted if used carefully. All interpretive claims, analyses, or [[WP:SYN|synthetic claims]] about primary sources must be referenced to a secondary source, rather than to [[WP:OR|original analysis]] of the primary-source material by Wikipedia editors.

:''Passed 9 to 0, 22:34, 24 August 2010 (UTC)''

===Mediation===
5) Mediation &mdash; whether [[WP:MEDCOM|formal]] or [[WP:MEDCAB|informal]] &mdash; is a voluntary process to help editors who are having a dispute.  While it serves the valuable function of facilitating agreement between good faith participants, it cannot make binding decisions on contents or sanction users.

:''Passed 10 to 0, 22:34, 24 August 2010 (UTC)''

===Advocacy===
6) Wikipedia strives towards a [[WP:NPOV|neutral point of view]].  Accordingly, it is not the appropriate venue for advocacy or for advancing a specific point of view.  While coverage of all significant points of view is a necessary part of balancing an article, striving to give exposure to minority viewpoints that are not significantly expressed in reliable secondary sources is not.

:''Passed 10 to 0, 22:34, 24 August 2010 (UTC)''

===Single purpose accounts===
7.1) [[WP:SPA|Single purpose accounts]] are expected to contribute neutrally instead of following their own agenda and, in particular, should take care to avoid creating the impression that their [[WP:SPA|focus on one topic]] is non-neutral, which could strongly suggest that their editing is not compatible with the goals of this project.

:''Passed 9 to 0, 22:34, 24 August 2010 (UTC)''

===Decorum===
8) Wikipedia users are expected to behave reasonably, calmly, and courteously in their interactions with other users; to approach even difficult situations in a dignified fashion and with a constructive and collaborative outlook; and to avoid acting in a manner that brings the project into disrepute. Unseemly conduct, such as [[Wikipedia:No personal attacks|personal attacks]], [[Wikipedia:Civility|incivility]], [[Wikipedia:Assume good faith|assumptions of bad faith]], [[Wikipedia:Harassment|harassment]], or [[Wikipedia:Do not disrupt Wikipedia to illustrate a point|disruptive point-making]], is prohibited.

:''Passed 9 to 0, 22:34, 24 August 2010 (UTC)''

===Lengthy evidence and sub-pages===

9) Longstanding consensus at [[Wikipedia:Miscellany for Deletion|Miscellany for Deletion]] is that editors may work up drafts in their userspace for the sole purpose of submitting the material as evidence in arbitration cases. However, after the case closes, the sub-pages should be courtesy-blanked or deleted as they are often perceived as [[WP:ATTACK|attack pages]] and serve only to memorialise and perpetuate the dispute. Evidence should properly be submitted only on arbitration pages as it is impossible to ensure that all the parties are aware of all the sub-pages that might have a bearing on them. If the evidence runs over the permitted length, it should not be continued on sub-pages but instead permission should be sought from the drafting arbitrator for an over-length submission.

:''Passed 9 to 0, 22:34, 24 August 2010 (UTC)''

===Tag-team editing===
10) Tag teams work in unison to push a [[WP:NPOV|particular point of view]]. Tag-team editing – to thwart core policies ([[WP:NPOV|neutral point of view]], [[WP:V|verifiability]], and [[WP:NOR|no original research]]); or to evade procedural restrictions such as the [[WP:3RR|three revert rule]] or to violate behavioural norms by [[Wikipedia:Edit war|edit warring]]; or to attempt to exert [[WP:OWN|ownership]] over articles; or otherwise to prevent [[WP:CONSENSUS|consensus]] prevailing – is prohibited.

:''Passed 6 to 0, 22:34, 24 August 2010 (UTC)''

==Findings of fact==
====Locus and focus of dispute====
1.1) The dispute is focused on articles within the [[:Category:Race and intelligence controversy|Race and intelligence controversy category]]. The core issue is whether [[Intelligence quotient]] varies significantly between different races and, if so, whether this may be attributed to genetic or environmental factors. The dispute may be characterised as comprising: (i) consistent [[WP:NPOV|point-of-view pushing]]; (ii) persistent [[WP:EW|edit-warring]]; and (iii) incessant [[WP:UNDUE|over-emphasis]] on certain controversial sources.

:''Passed 9 to 0, 22:34, 24 August 2010 (UTC)''

===Informal mediation===
4) During the dispute, some (but not all) of the participants have engaged in informal mediation[http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Wikipedia:Mediation_Cabal/Cases/2009-11-12/Race_and_Intelligence&oldid=355480402] with the [[WP:MEDCAB|Mediation Cabal]].  While well-intended, that attempt at mediation was fundamentally flawed because it purported to create a binding decision and proceeded despite major participants having refused mediation.

:''Passed 10 to 0, 22:34, 24 August 2010 (UTC)''

